window.SUBJECTS = window.SUBJECTS || [];
window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"bio","n":"Biology","tab":"Biology","w":"Cells and genetics, the human body, health, plants and the living world.","topics":[{"id":"cell","n":"Cell, Genetics and DNA","qs":[{"id":"q0037","q":"Turner’s syndrome results due to:","o":["gain of extra copy of Y chromosome in human males","loss of a Y chromosome in human males","gain of extra copy of X chromosome in human females","loss of an X chromosome in human females"],"a":3,"x":"Turner's syndrome (45, X0) is a female with one X chromosome missing: short stature, underdeveloped ovaries, sterility.","e":"Klinefelter's syndrome is the opposite case: a male with an extra X (47, XXY).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0160","q":"Which of the following is NOT a salient feature of genetic code?","o":["AUG has dual functions.","The code is nearly universal.","Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon.","The codon is quadruplet."],"a":3,"x":"The genetic code is a triplet: three bases make one codon. It is nearly universal and degenerate (several codons for one amino acid), and AUG both codes methionine and starts translation.","e":"64 codons in all: 61 code for amino acids, 3 are stop codons.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0181","q":"The approximate size of the human genome is about:","o":["3000 Mb","4000 Mb","5000 Mb","6000 Mb"],"a":0,"x":"The human genome has about 3.2 billion base pairs, which is about 3,000 Mb (megabases).","e":"The Human Genome Project finished its sequence in 2003; there are about 20,000 protein-coding genes.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0228","q":"The number of codons that code for 20 amino acids is:","o":["55","58","61","64"],"a":2,"x":"Of the 64 codons, 61 code for the 20 amino acids and 3 (UAA, UAG, UGA) are stop codons.","e":"AUG codes methionine and is also the start codon.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0232","q":"According to Mendel, the factor responsible for the inheritance of specific traits is _________.","o":["chromosomes","factors","DNA","genes"],"a":3,"x":"Mendel called the units of inheritance 'factors'; today they are called genes, a word coined by Johannsen in 1909.","e":"Mendel worked with garden peas and gave the laws of dominance, segregation and independent assortment.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"],"k":"Mendel's own word was 'factors'; DSSSB's key marks the modern term, genes."},{"id":"q0308","q":"By which process is the information in RNA used to make proteins?","o":["Replication","Mutation","Synthesis","Translation"],"a":3,"x":"Translation is the process in which the ribosome reads mRNA and builds a protein from its codons.","e":"The central dogma: DNA → (transcription) → RNA → (translation) → protein.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0349","q":"The process by which genetic information is copied from one strand of the DNA into RNA termed as _______.","o":["transcription","replication","mutation","translation"],"a":0,"x":"Transcription copies genetic information from one DNA strand into RNA; translation then uses the RNA to build protein.","e":"Replication copies DNA into DNA.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0390","q":"Which of the following represents an autosomal dominant trait in a representative pedigree analysis (e.g., myotonic dystrophy)?","o":["Trait skips generations and appears only in males","Trait affects only males and is passed from father to son","Trait appears in every generation and affects both sexes equally","Trait appears only in offspring of carrier mothers"],"a":2,"x":"An autosomal dominant trait shows up in every generation and affects males and females equally; one faulty copy is enough.","e":"Myotonic dystrophy and Huntington's disease are autosomal dominant.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0436","q":"Which of the following combinations is correct?","o":["Sickle-cell anaemia – mutation on chromosome 11","Thalassaemia – X-linked inherited disorder","Down syndrome – only 45 chromosomes","Haemophilia – Y-linked inherited disorder"],"a":0,"x":"Sickle-cell anaemia is caused by a mutation in the β-globin gene on chromosome 11. Thalassaemia is autosomal (not X-linked), Down syndrome has 47 chromosomes, and haemophilia is X-linked.","e":"In sickle-cell anaemia one amino acid changes: glutamic acid → valine.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1601","q":"The dense circular body part in the center of a cell is known as:","o":["Cell Membrane","Nucleus","Cell Wall","Cell tissue"],"a":1,"x":"The nucleus is the dense, usually round body at the centre of a cell that controls its activities and holds the DNA.","e":"Robert Brown discovered the nucleus in 1831.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0998","q":"The last amino acid in a protein sequence is called the _______.","o":["N-terminal","Beta-end","C-terminal","Omega group"],"a":2,"x":"A protein chain runs from the N-terminal (free amino group, first amino acid) to the C-terminal (free carboxyl group, last amino acid).","e":"Ribosomes build proteins from the N-terminal towards the C-terminal.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1530","q":"The biological process by which traits pass from one generation to the next is called __________.","o":["Heredity","Regeneration","Mutation","Transformation"],"a":0,"x":"Heredity is the passing of traits from parents to offspring through genes.","e":"Genetics is the study of heredity and variation; Mendel is its father.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]}]},{"id":"human","n":"Human Body","qs":[{"id":"q0107","q":"What type of tissue is blood made up of?","o":["Epithelial","Muscular","Connective","Meristematic"],"a":2,"x":"Blood is a fluid connective tissue: plasma is its matrix and it carries red cells, white cells and platelets.","e":"Other connective tissues: bone, cartilage, tendon, ligament, adipose (fat).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0156","q":"What is the number of neurons found in the human brain?","o":["~16 billion","~86 billion","~16 million","~86 million"],"a":1,"x":"The human brain has about 86 billion neurons.","e":"The cerebellum holds most of them, although the cerebrum is larger.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0187","q":"The respiratory rhythm is primarily regulated by the respiratory centre located in the _______.","o":["medulla region","cerebellum region","cerebrum region","hypothalamus region"],"a":0,"x":"The respiratory rhythm centre is in the medulla oblongata; the pneumotaxic centre in the pons fine-tunes it.","e":"The medulla also controls heartbeat, swallowing and vomiting.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0275","q":"What is the function of the epiglottis during swallowing?","o":["To moisten the air","To produce sound","To help in breathing","To prevent food from entering the larynx"],"a":3,"x":"The epiglottis is a flap that closes over the larynx when you swallow, so food goes into the oesophagus and not the windpipe.","e":"Food 'going down the wrong pipe' is what happens when it fails.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0386","q":"The hormone that regulates blood sugar levels in the human body is:","o":["TSH","Estrogen","Insulin","Thyroxine"],"a":2,"x":"Insulin, from the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas, lowers blood sugar; glucagon from alpha cells raises it.","e":"Too little insulin causes diabetes mellitus.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0479","q":"The approximate amount of CO2 s directly dissolved in plasma is:","o":["0-3%","5-10%","15-25%","40-60%"],"a":1,"x":"About 7% (5–10%) of carbon dioxide is carried dissolved in plasma; about 70% goes as bicarbonate and about 20–25% bound to haemoglobin.","e":"Carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells speeds up bicarbonate formation.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0610","q":"Which of these organs helps in the filtration of blood?","o":["Kidney","Lungs","Gallbladder","Larynx"],"a":0,"x":"The kidneys filter blood: nephrons remove urea and excess salt and water to form urine.","e":"Each kidney has about a million nephrons.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0690","q":"___________ is the chemical process in which different types of chemical reactions involved in controlling the living state of the cells in an organism.","o":["Metabolism","Respiration","Transportation","Reproduction"],"a":0,"x":"Metabolism is the sum of all chemical reactions that keep a cell or organism alive: anabolism builds molecules and catabolism breaks them down.","e":"Respiration is one catabolic process.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0691","q":"The lens focuses light on the back of the eye. The back of the eye is known as _____________.","o":["cornea","iris","lens","retina"],"a":3,"x":"The lens focuses light on the retina at the back of the eye, where rods and cones turn light into signals to the brain.","e":"Rods handle dim light; cones handle colour.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1449","q":"Glomerulonephritis are part of human ______ system.","o":["reproductive","respiratory","excretory","circulatory"],"a":2,"x":"Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli, the filtering units of the kidney, so it belongs to the excretory system.","e":"The glomerulus sits inside Bowman's capsule of the nephron.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q0818","q":"Which part of the human ear helps in the sensation of sound?","o":["Lungs","Semicircular canal","Retina","Cochlea"],"a":3,"x":"The cochlea, the snail-shaped part of the inner ear, turns sound vibrations into nerve signals for hearing. The semicircular canals handle balance.","e":"The auditory nerve carries these signals to the brain.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"health","n":"Nutrition and Diseases","qs":[{"id":"q0031","q":"Which of the following statements about vitamins is/are correct? Statement A: With the help of Vitamin B the body's cells are functioning properly. Vitamin B also helps the body convert food into energy. Statement B: Vitamin A is important for normal vision, the immune system, and reproduction. Statement C: Vitamin K helps the body absorb and retain iron and protein.","o":["Only A and C","Only B and C","Only A and B","A, B and C"],"a":2,"x":"A and B are correct. C is wrong: Vitamin K is needed for blood clotting. Absorbing iron is helped by Vitamin C.","e":"The K comes from the German 'Koagulation' — clotting.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0078","q":"Which groups of people are recommended zinc supplementation by World Health Organization (WHO) and United Nations International Children's Emergency Fund (UNICEF)?","o":["Low-income elderly persons","Children with severe malnutrition or diarrhoea","Non-lactating women","All children in low-income areas where stunting is common"],"a":1,"x":"WHO and UNICEF recommend zinc, with ORS, for children with diarrhoea, and for severely malnourished children, because zinc shortens the illness and helps recovery.","e":"Standard advice: ORS plus 10–14 days of zinc for childhood diarrhoea.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0237","q":"In the context of diet and nutrition, what is the full form of RDA?","o":["Regular Dietary Allowances","Recommended Dietary Allowances","Required Diet Amount","Routine Diet Amount"],"a":1,"x":"RDA means Recommended Dietary Allowances: the daily intake of nutrients enough for nearly all healthy people.","e":"In India, ICMR–NIN sets RDAs.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0426","q":"Which element is necessary for the formation of haemoglobin?","o":["Iron","Zinc","Calcium","Magnesium"],"a":0,"x":"Iron is needed to make haemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying pigment of red blood cells; lack of it causes anaemia.","e":"Good sources: green leafy vegetables, jaggery, liver; Vitamin C helps absorption.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0472","q":"Which of the following is NOT a primary source of simple carbohydrate?","o":["Milk","Potato","Wheat","Meat"],"a":3,"x":"Meat is mainly protein and fat; milk (lactose), potato (starch) and wheat (starch) supply carbohydrates.","e":"Simple carbohydrates: glucose, fructose, lactose, sucrose.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1603","q":"A lack of iodine in food causes ______.","o":["Goitre","Fever","Malaria","Cancer"],"a":0,"x":"Iodine deficiency causes goitre (swelling of the thyroid), since iodine is needed to make thyroxine.","e":"India's iodised salt programme fights iodine deficiency.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0654","q":"What is the scientific name of Vitamin B6?","o":["Cobalamin","Pyridoxine","Calciferol","Phytomenadione"],"a":1,"x":"Vitamin B6 is pyridoxine.","e":"B1 thiamine, B2 riboflavin, B3 niacin, B12 cobalamin; D calciferol; K phylloquinone.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0694","q":"Rickets is caused due to the deficiency of which of the following vitamins?","o":["Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"a":3,"x":"Rickets, soft and bent bones in children, comes from Vitamin D deficiency; in adults it is osteomalacia.","e":"The skin makes Vitamin D in sunlight.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0734","q":"Which one of the following describes the term \"hypochondria\"?","o":["Sleep with hallucinations","Excessive snoring","Daytime sleeping","Anxiety about state of health"],"a":3,"x":"Hypochondria (illness anxiety disorder) is excessive worry about having a serious illness despite little or no evidence.","e":"Insomnia is a sleep disorder; hallucinations are false perceptions.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1299","q":"Which of the following foods has the highest percentage of proteins per unit mass?","o":["Egg","Meat","Cow-milk","Fish"],"a":1,"x":"Of these, meat has the highest protein per unit mass (about 25–30% in lean cooked meat), ahead of fish, eggs (about 13%) and milk (about 3.5%).","e":"Plant sources rich in protein: soybean (about 36–40%), pulses.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1053","q":"Which of the following is/are not classified as (a) nutrient(s)?","o":["Vitamins","Hormones","Proteins","Water"],"a":1,"x":"Hormones are chemical messengers made by the body; they are not nutrients. The six nutrient groups: carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals and water.","e":"Insulin, thyroxine and adrenaline are hormones.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1392","q":"Which of the following is NOT a sexually transmitted disease?","o":["AIDS","Amoebiasis","Gonorrhea","Syphilis"],"a":1,"x":"Amoebiasis (amoebic dysentery), caused by Entamoeba histolytica, spreads through contaminated food and water, not sexually. AIDS, gonorrhoea and syphilis are STDs.","e":"Houseflies act as mechanical carriers of amoebiasis.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1411","q":"Which of the following is a vector borne disease?","o":["Diabetes","Breast Cancer","Covid-19","Malaria"],"a":3,"x":"Malaria is a vector-borne disease: the Plasmodium parasite is carried by the female Anopheles mosquito.","e":"Other vector-borne diseases: dengue and chikungunya (Aedes), kala-azar (sand fly).","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1426","q":"In which of the following diseases does a parasite develop in the red blood cells (RBCs) of humans?","o":["Covid-19","Avian influenza","Dengue","Malaria"],"a":3,"x":"In malaria the Plasmodium parasite multiplies inside human red blood cells, bursting them and causing cycles of fever.","e":"The parasite first multiplies in the liver.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1478","q":"Which of the following is NOT a rich source of Vitamin A?","o":["Carrot","Mango","Amla","Papaya"],"a":2,"x":"Amla (Indian gooseberry) is famous for Vitamin C, not Vitamin A. Carrot, mango and papaya are rich in beta-carotene, which gives Vitamin A.","e":"Lack of Vitamin A causes night blindness.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1498","q":"Which of the following diseases, usually caused by vitamin B1 deficiency, exhibits loss of muscle function or paralysis of lower limbs as common symptoms?","o":["Beri-beri","Scurvy","Pellagra","Pernicious anemia"],"a":0,"x":"Beri-beri, from Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency, causes muscle weakness, nerve damage and even paralysis of the legs.","e":"Scurvy: Vitamin C. Pellagra: B3 (niacin). Pernicious anaemia: B12.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1513","q":"According to 2025 updates, what is the causative agent of Trachoma, the eye disease India has eliminated?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Chlamydia trachomatis","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Escherichia coli"],"a":1,"x":"Trachoma is an eye infection caused by the bacterium Chlamydia trachomatis; repeated infection can cause blindness. WHO declared India free of trachoma as a public-health problem in 2024.","e":"It spreads through flies and contact with eye discharge.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0847","q":"What is the full form of AIDS?","o":["Acquired Identity Deficiency Syndrome","Associated Immuno Deficiency Syndrome","Associated Identity Deficiency Syndrome","Acquired Immuno Deficiency Syndrome"],"a":3,"x":"AIDS stands for Acquired Immuno Deficiency Syndrome, caused by HIV, which destroys the CD4 helper T-cells of the immune system.","e":"World AIDS Day: 1 December. ELISA is the screening test.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0855","q":"Which demographic indicator showed an increase from NFHS-4 (2015–16) to NFHS-5 (2019–21)?","o":["Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)","Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)","Anaemia among women (15–49 years)","Total Fertility Rate (TFR)"],"a":2,"x":"NFHS-5 (2019–21) found anaemia among women aged 15–49 rose to about 57%, from 53% in NFHS-4; infant mortality and fertility fell.","e":"India's total fertility rate fell to 2.0, below replacement level.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0860","q":"Which of the following is a major mineral needed by the human body?","o":["Molybdenum","Selenium","Chromium","Potassium"],"a":3,"x":"Potassium is a major mineral (macromineral) the body needs in large amounts for nerve impulses, muscle contraction and fluid balance. Molybdenum, selenium and chromium are trace minerals.","e":"Other macrominerals: calcium, phosphorus, sodium, magnesium.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]}]},{"id":"plants","n":"Plants","qs":[{"id":"q0120","q":"Identify the correct sequence from the outer to the inner side in the transverse section of a plant root.","o":["Epidermis – xylem – endodermis – pericycle – cortex","Epidermis – xylem – endodermis – cortex – pericycle","Epidermis – xylem – endodermis – pericycle","Epidermis – cortex – endodermis – pericycle – xylem"],"a":3,"x":"From outside in, a root cross-section shows epidermis, cortex, endodermis, pericycle, then the vascular tissue (xylem and phloem).","e":"Lateral roots grow from the pericycle.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0152","q":"The hormone auxin is primarily associated with _________.","o":["chlorophyll formation","seed storage","flowering in winter","cell elongation"],"a":3,"x":"Auxin causes cell elongation, which makes shoots bend towards light (phototropism); it also promotes rooting and apical dominance.","e":"Gibberellin: stem lengthening. Cytokinin: cell division. Abscisic acid: dormancy, the stress hormone. Ethylene: fruit ripening.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0186","q":"Which plant division is known for its unique reproductive structure called the 'cone'?","o":["Angiosperms","Pteridophyta","Bryophyta","Gymnosperms"],"a":3,"x":"Gymnosperms carry naked seeds on cones (strobili); pines, cycads and Ginkgo belong here.","e":"Angiosperms enclose seeds in fruits.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0314","q":"What is the function of the ovary in a flower?","o":["Produce seeds","Support the anther","Produce pollen","Attract pollinators"],"a":0,"x":"The ovary holds the ovules. After fertilisation the ovules become seeds and the ovary becomes the fruit, so the ovary is where seeds are produced.","e":"The anther makes pollen; petals attract pollinators.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0344","q":"Stolon that helps the plants in asexual reproduction is the modified form of the ____________.","o":["stem","root","leaf","bud"],"a":0,"x":"A stolon (runner) is a modified stem that grows along the ground and gives rise to new plants at its nodes.","e":"Examples: strawberry, grass. Potato tubers and ginger rhizomes are also modified stems.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0350","q":"Which of the following is a major function of auxin in plants?","o":["Inhibiting seed growth","Promoting cell elongation","Promoting leaf fall","Stimulating root decay"],"a":1,"x":"Auxin's key job is promoting cell elongation, which drives shoot growth and bending towards light.","e":"Synthetic auxins such as 2,4-D are used as weedkillers.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q1622","q":"What is the botanical name of coffee plant?","o":["Coffea austinian","Coffea arabica","Coffea africanus","Coffea australiana"],"a":1,"x":"The main coffee plant is Coffea arabica (Arabica); Coffea canephora (Robusta) is the other major species.","e":"India mostly grows Arabica and Robusta in Karnataka, Kerala and Tamil Nadu.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0532","q":"Ferns belongs to which of the following divisions of the plant kingdom?","o":["Gymnosperms","Angiosperms","Thallophyta","Pteridophyta"],"a":3,"x":"Ferns belong to Pteridophyta, the first land plants with vascular tissue (xylem and phloem), which reproduce by spores.","e":"Other pteridophytes: horsetails (Equisetum), Selaginella, Marsilea.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0577","q":"The cultivation of trees and shrubs is known as _______________.","o":["Viticulture","Pomiculture","Arboriculture","Pisciculture"],"a":2,"x":"Arboriculture is the cultivation and care of trees and shrubs.","e":"Viticulture: grapes. Pomiculture: fruits. Pisciculture: fish.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0612","q":"Which among the plant kingdoms is also known as the amphibians of the plant kingdom?","o":["Bryophyte","Thallophyte","Gymnosperms","Angiosperms"],"a":0,"x":"Bryophytes (mosses, liverworts) are the 'amphibians of the plant kingdom': they live on land but need water for fertilisation.","e":"Examples: Funaria (moss), Marchantia (liverwort).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0732","q":"One of the following is classified as a flowering plant. Name it.","o":["Grasses","Conifers","Ferns","Mosses"],"a":0,"x":"Grasses are flowering plants (angiosperms, monocots). Conifers are gymnosperms, ferns are pteridophytes, and mosses are bryophytes.","e":"Wheat, rice, maize and bamboo are all grasses.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1293","q":"What is the absorption of water by plant roots an example of?","o":["Photosynthesis","Endocytosis","Osmosis","Transplantation"],"a":2,"x":"Root hairs absorb water by osmosis: water moves from the dilute soil water into the more concentrated cell sap across a semi-permeable membrane.","e":"Minerals are taken up mostly by active transport, which uses energy.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1327","q":"The members of chlorophyceae are commonly called which of the following algaes?","o":["brown","red","blue","green"],"a":3,"x":"Chlorophyceae are green algae: their main pigments are chlorophyll a and b, and they store starch.","e":"Examples: Chlamydomonas, Spirogyra, Chlorella.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1041","q":"Leaves characterized by parallel venation are a distinctive feature of:","o":["Gymnosperms","Monocots","Pteridophytes","Dicots"],"a":1,"x":"Parallel venation in leaves is typical of monocots, such as grasses, wheat, maize and banana. Dicots usually have net (reticulate) venation.","e":"Monocots also have fibrous roots and flower parts in threes.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1545","q":"Agar is obtained from ______________.","o":["Aspergillus","Algae","Agaricus","Fungi"],"a":1,"x":"Agar is a jelly-like substance obtained from red algae such as Gelidium and Gracilaria, used in labs and food.","e":"Carrageenan also comes from red algae; algin from brown algae.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1546","q":"Palm trees belong to which plant family?","o":["Arecaceae, a family of monocot flowering plants","Fabaceae, a family of leguminous flowering plants","Moraceae, a family of tropical flowering plants","Pinaceae, a family of coniferous evergreen plants"],"a":0,"x":"Palms belong to the Arecaceae family of monocot flowering plants.","e":"The coconut and the date palm are also Arecaceae.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"living","n":"Classification and Animals","qs":[{"id":"q0118","q":"Identify the shape which is NOT found in the kingdom Monera.","o":["Bacilli","Vibrio","Cocci","Heterocyst"],"a":3,"x":"Bacteria (kingdom Monera) come in shapes such as cocci (round), bacilli (rods), vibrio (comma) and spirilla (spiral). A heterocyst is a nitrogen-fixing cell in some cyanobacteria, not a shape.","e":"Anabaena and Nostoc have heterocysts.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0494","q":"Earthworms belongs to which of the following phyla?","o":["Platyhelminthes","Annelida","Porifera","Arthropods"],"a":1,"x":"Earthworms belong to phylum Annelida: segmented worms with ring-like segments.","e":"Other annelids: leech, Nereis.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0570","q":"Bacteria belongs to which of the following Kingdoms?","o":["Plantae","Fungi","Protista","Monera"],"a":3,"x":"Bacteria belong to kingdom Monera in Whittaker's five-kingdom classification (1969): prokaryotes with no nuclear membrane.","e":"The five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0652","q":"Starfish belongs to which of the following phyla?","o":["Echinodermata","Platyhelminthes","Annelida","Porifera"],"a":0,"x":"Starfish belong to phylum Echinodermata: spiny-skinned marine animals with a water vascular system and tube feet.","e":"Other echinoderms: sea urchin, sea cucumber, brittle star. Despite the name, a starfish is not a fish.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0693","q":"Parrot belongs to which of the following classes of vertebrates?","o":["Reptilla","Amphibia","Aves","Pisces"],"a":2,"x":"Parrots belong to class Aves (birds): feathered, warm-blooded vertebrates that lay eggs.","e":"Other vertebrate classes: Pisces, Amphibia, Reptilia, Mammalia.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1298","q":"Which of the following organism-classification does Anabaena belong to?","o":["Monera","Protista","Plantae","Fungi"],"a":0,"x":"Anabaena is a cyanobacterium (blue-green alga) and so belongs to kingdom Monera; it fixes nitrogen in its heterocysts.","e":"Anabaena lives with the water fern Azolla, used as a biofertiliser in rice fields.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1338","q":"Which among the following phyla is also known as sea walnuts?","o":["Ctenophora","Coelenterata","Platyhelminthes","Porifera"],"a":0,"x":"Ctenophores, the comb jellies, are called sea walnuts; they swim with eight rows of ciliated comb plates and show bioluminescence.","e":"Example: Pleurobrachia.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0766","q":"Which of the following bacteria shows a 'Chinese letter' arrangement of cells?","o":["Clostridium tetani","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Bacillus anthracis"],"a":2,"x":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae, which causes diphtheria, is seen under the microscope in a 'Chinese letter' arrangement of rod-shaped cells.","e":"Diphtheria is prevented by the DPT vaccine.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]}]}]});
